    "sha": "3683ebd6888671a9b632fe93d3905a7cd9ca58b2",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVoU8JdoAKDM2ODNlYmQ2ODg4NjcxYTliNjMyZmU5M2QzOTA1YTdjZDljYTU4YjI",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-25T03:46:31Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-25T03:46:31Z"
      },
      "message": "perf(sim): section a prismatic Z slab once for a ramped path\n\nA keep-down ramp ends each move at a Z of its own. The verifier sectioned\nthe model at each of those planes, probed each region edge of each move\nagainst the whole section, and gave the reach law the wall samples of each\nplane. On a plate with 38,432 facets one ring took 282 s (release).\n\n- verify/slab.rs: the Z ranges between two vertex levels where each\n  crossing facet is vertical. Each plane of such a slab is the same point\n  set. Rest::island_slice gives those planes one shared section. A plane\n  that the path cuts flat at keeps its own section.\n- reach law (c): a shared slab section is sampled along its walls each\n  0.25 mm, so a ramp reads a wall along its length as it did with one\n  section for each plane.\n- gouge law (b): the distance test comes before the wall probe. The two\n  tests are pure, so no result changes.\n\nThe same case takes 1.2 s with the same moves and the same warnings.\n\nFixes #3831\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
